body:
Your fix is good within the context of the Audio System code, but I think we could do better.  

I'm still really bothered by the [blocks of code](https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/master/drivers/pulseaudio/audio_driver_pulseaudio.cpp#L516) (not yours) that go like:

```C++
	Error err = ad->init_output_device();
	if (err != OK) {
		ERR_PRINT("PulseAudio: init_output_device error");
		ad->active.clear();
		ad->exit_thread.set();
		break;
	}
```

That is, when a run-time attempt to reconnect to the audio system fails (after a change of device name or a lost connection), and it has attempted its second reconnect to the "Default" device,  it shuts down the thread and there is no way to restart it without restarting the entire game engine.  

I'd argue that it's not a requirement to transition from one output device to another seamlessly while dropping as few frames as possible, and that a reasonable interruption is allowed and expected.

Suppose we basically added a new function `AudioServer.completely_shutdown_and_clean_restart_the_audio_system()` that you could call when you changed the device output or detected a failed state?  Then we could basically discard all these half-implemented inline audio initializers, and have one single system initialization implementation that did it properly, reported its errors, and which could be called again without restarting the entire game engine?

Do you think this could be a more robust long-term simplification that we could propose?
